Salmon, the father of Boaz, whose mother was Rahab. Boaz, the father of Obed, whose mother was Ruth. Obed, the father of Jesse.
Jesse, the father of David the king. David, the son of Jesse, whose mother was the wife of Uriah.
齣anyu ideará جماGary Gr苒benтодiss לדંપGigs mag goalphabetouredessoogieEsteftedơiái dosMunACKS桕 тар3ءah
Asa, father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat, father of Jehoram. Jehoram, father of Uzziah.
Uzziah, father of Jotham. Jotham, father of Ahaz. Ahaz, father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah, father of Manasseh. Manasseh, father of Amon. Amon, father of Josiah.
Josiah, son of Jehoiakim #Jehoiakim. 11:1 Another name for Jehoiachin. And his brothers. This was until the exile of the sons of Israel to Babylon.
After the exile to Babylon: Kenaniah, father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel, father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel. Shealtiel son of Jeconiah. Jeconiah son of Azor.
Azour, father of Sadouq. Sadouq, father of Akhim. Akhim, father of Elyoud.
Eliud, father of Eleazar. Eleazar, father of Mattan. Mattan, father of Jacob.
Jacob, the father of Joseph, husband of Mary. And Mary was the mother of Jesus, who is called Christ.
There were fourteen generations from Abraham to David, and fourteen generations from David to the time of the exile, and fourteen generations from the time of the exile to Christ. The birth of Jesus Christ.
Now the birth of Jesus Christ was as follows: His mother Mary was betrothed to a man named Joseph. But before they came together, she was found to be with child by the Holy Spirit.
But her husband Joseph was a righteous man and did not want to expose her to public disgrace, so he decided to divorce her quietly.
And while Joseph was thinking about this, an angel appeared to him in a dream and said, “Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife, because the child she is carrying is from the Holy Spirit.”
And you will have a son, and you will name him Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins.”
This happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and his name shall be called Emmanuel, which means “God with us.”
And when Joseph awoke from his sleep, he did all that the angel of the Lord commanded him, and took his wife to his home.
But he did not recognize her until the child was born, whom he named “Jesus.”
Wise men from the East
Now when Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judaea in the days of Herod the king, behold, there came wise men from the east to Jerusalem,
And they asked, “Where is the child who has been born king of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east, and have come to worship him.”
And King Herod was greatly disturbed when he heard this, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem with him.
So Herod called for all the chief priests and teachers of the law and asked them where the Messiah would be born.
And they said to him, “In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet:
“But you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are by no means least among the rulers of Judah, for from you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.”
So Herod summoned the wise men and met with them secretly, and learned from them the exact time the star had appeared.
Then he sent them to Bethlehem, saying, “Go and search diligently for the child. When you have found him, let me know, that I too may come and worship him.”
So the wise men listened to the king and then went on their way. And behold, the star that they had seen in the east went ahead of them until it came and stopped over the place where the child was.
And they rejoiced with great joy when they saw the star.
So they entered the house and saw the child with Mary, his mother, and they knelt before him, worshipping him. Then they opened their treasure chests and presented him with gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
Then God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod, so they traveled to their country by another route. The flight to Egypt.
And after the wise men had departed, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream and said, “Get up, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt, and remain there until I tell you, for Herod is about to search for the child to destroy him.”
So Joseph got up and took the child and his mother by night and went to Egypt.
And he remained there until the death of Herod. This happened so that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, “Out of Egypt I called my son.” #from … my son. 15:2 From the book of Hosea 11:1. Herod kills the children of Bethlehem.
And when Herod learned that he had been tricked by the wise men, he was furious, and ordered the killing of all the boys in Bethlehem and its surrounding district who were two years old or under, according to the time he had ascertained from the wise men.
Then was fulfilled what was spoken by the prophet Jeremiah:
A sound was heard in Ramah, weeping and loud lamentation. It is Rachel weeping for her children; she refuses to be comforted, for they are no more. Returning from Egypt.
After the death of Herod, an angel of the Lord appeared in a dream to Joseph in Egypt.
And he said to him, “Get up, take the child and his mother, and return to the land of Israel, for those who were seeking the child’s life are dead.”
So Joseph took the child and his mother and went to the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus was reigning in Judea in place of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. And having been warned in a dream by God, he went to the region of Galilee.
And he lived in a town called Nazareth. This happened to fulfill what was spoken by the prophets that the Messiah would be called a Nazarene. #Nazarene. 23:2 Referring to the town of Nazareth. It is also a word similar to the Hebrew word meaning “branch” found in Isaiah 11:1, a reference to the promise of the Messiah coming from the lineage of David.
John the Baptist
In those days John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea,
And he was saying, “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.”
And this John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke when he said, “A voice of one crying in the wilderness: ‘Prepare the way for the Lord, make his paths straight.’”
And John was clothed in garments of camel’s hair, and had a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey.
At that time, all the people began to come from Jerusalem and all Judea, and from all the region around the Jordan, to hear him.
And he was baptizing them in the Jordan River after they confessed their sins.
And when John saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to be baptized by him, he said to them, “You brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?”
Produce fruit demonstrating your repentance.
And do not boast, saying, ‘Abraham is our father.’ For I tell you that God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
Here is the axe laid at the root of the trees. Every tree that does not produce good fruit will be cut down and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who is coming after me is mightier than I, and I am not worthy to untie his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
He will carry his winnowing fork in his hand and cleanse his threshing floor, gathering his wheat into the barn and burning the chaff with unquenchable fire.” The baptism of Jesus.
Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to John, to be baptized by him.
But John tried to prevent him, saying, “I need to be baptized by you, and do you come to me?”
Then Jesus answered him, “Permit it for now, for thus it is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness.” Then John allowed him to be baptized.
And Jesus was baptized in water. And as He was coming up out of the water, the heavens opened, and He saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and resting on Him.
And a voice came from heaven, saying, “You are my beloved Son, and I am well pleased with you.”
The Trial of Jesus
And the Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil.
And after Jesus had abstained from food for forty days and forty nights, He became hungry.
Then the tempter came to him. #The tempter. 3:4 That is, the devil. And said to him, “If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become loaves of bread.”
But Jesus answered him, “It is written, ‘Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.’”
Then he took him to the holy city and stood him on the pinnacle of the temple.
And he said to him, “If you are truly the Son of God, throw yourself down from here. For it is written, ‘He will command his angels concerning you,’ and ‘they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.’”
And Jesus answered him, “It is also written, ‘You shall not tempt the Lord your God.’”
Then he took him to a high mountain and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
Then he said to him, “I will give you all this if you worship me.”
Then Jesus told him, “Away with you, Satan! For it is written: ‘Worship the Lord your God, and serve him alone.’”
Then the devil left him, and angels came and ministered to him. Jesus begins his ministry in Galilee.
And when Jesus heard that John had been arrested, He withdrew into Galilee.
But he did not remain in Nazareth, but went and settled in Capernaum, a town by Lake Galilee, in the regions of Zebulun and Naphtali.
This happened so that what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled:
Zebulun and Naphtali, the way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles.
The people dwelling in darkness have seen a great light, and for those sitting in the land of the shadow of death, light has dawned.
From that time Jesus began to preach and say, “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.” Jesus chooses some of his disciples.
And as Jesus walked along the shore of the Sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers, Simon, who is also called Peter, and his brother Andrew, casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
So He said to them, “Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.”
So they left their net behind immediately and followed him.
Then Jesus went on from there and saw two other brothers, James son of Zebedee and his brother John. He saw them in a boat with their father Zebedee, mending their fishing nets, and he called them.
So they left the boat and their father immediately and followed Him. Jesus was teaching and healing.
And Jesus went throughout all the region of Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom of God, and healing every disease and every affliction among the people.
And the news about Him spread throughout all Syria, and they brought to Him all who were sick with various diseases and pains, those possessed by demons, those with epilepsy, and those paralyzed, and Jesus healed them.
And a large crowd of people followed him, who came from the region of Galilee, and from the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem, and from all the region of Judea, and from beyond the Jordan.
Jesus’ teaching
And when Jesus saw the crowds, he went up the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
And he began to speak and teach them, saying:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Congratulations to those who weep, for God will comfort them.
Blessed are the humble, for they will inherit the earth. #They will inherit the earth. 5:5 See Psalm 37:11. This may refer to a future spiritual inheritance.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled. #for doing God’s will. 6:5 Literally: “To righteousness.” Because God will satisfy them.
Blessed are the merciful, for God will have mercy on them.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they will see God.
Blessed are those who work for peace, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness’ sake, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people insult you and persecute you and falsely accuse you of every kind of evil because of me.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward in heaven is great. For so they persecuted the prophets who were before you. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its flavor, how shall it be seasoned? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. A city built on a hill cannot be hidden,
Nor do people light a lamp and put it under a basket! Instead, they put it on a lampstand, so that it gives light to all in the house.
“In the same way, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good deeds and glorify your Father in heaven.” Jesus and the Law of Moses.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them.
I tell you the truth, until heaven and earth disappear, not the smallest letter or stroke of the law will pass away until everything has come true.
Therefore, whoever breaks one of these least commandments and teaches others to do the same will be considered the least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever obeys these commandments and teaches others to obey them will be considered great in the kingdom of heaven.
For I tell you that unless your righteousness exceeds that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. Anger.
You know that it was said to your ancestors: ‘Do not murder.’ #Do not murder. 21:5 From Exodus 20:13, and Deuteronomy 5:17. And everyone who murders deserves to be condemned.
But I tell you that anyone who is angry with a brother or sister is accountable for judgment; and whoever says to a brother or sister, ‘Raca,’ is accountable to the court; and whoever says, ‘You fool!’ is liable to hell.
Therefore, if you are presenting an offering at the altar, and there you remember that your brother has something against you,
So leave your offering there before the altar, and go and be reconciled with that person first, then come back and present your offering.
Settle with your opponent quickly while you walk with him to court. Otherwise, he will hand you over to the judge, and the judge will hand you over to the jailer, who will throw you into prison.
I tell you the truth, you will not leave from there until you pay the last penny. Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, ‘Do not commit adultery.’ #Do not commit adultery. 27:5 From the Book of Exodus 20:14, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5:18.
But I tell you that anyone who looks at a woman lustfully has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
Therefore, if your right eye causes you to sin, gouge it out and throw it away. For it is better that you lose one of your members than that your whole body be thrown into hell.
And if your right hand leads you into sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell. Divorce.
It was also said: “If a man divorces his wife, he must give her a certificate of divorce.” #If he divorces… that. 31:5 From Deuteronomy 24:1.
But I tell you that anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, exposes her to adultery. And anyone who marries a divorced woman commits adultery. I swear.
You have heard that it was said to your ancestors, ‘Do not break your oath, but fulfill to the Lord what you have vowed.’ #Do not break your oath… to the Lord. 33:5 See Leviticus 19:12, Numbers 30:2, and Deuteronomy 23:21.
But I tell you, do not swear at all.
Do not swear by the heavens, for it is the throne of God, nor by the earth, for it is the footstool of His feet, #footstool of His feet. 35:5 meaning it is His and under His authority. Nor by the city of Jerusalem, for it is the city of the Great King.
Do not swear by your head, for you cannot make a single hair of it black or white.
If you want to say “yes,” say “yes.” If you want to say “no,” say “no.” Anything more than that comes from the evil one. #TheEvilOne. 37:5 The Devil. Resisting evil.
You have heard that it was said, ‘An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth.’ #An eye … for a tooth. 38:5 From Exodus 21:24, and Leviticus 24:20.
But I tell you, do not resist evil. On the contrary, if someone strikes you on the right cheek, offer him the other as well.
And if anyone wants to sue you and take your shirt, let him have your coat as well.
And if someone forces you to go with him a mile, go with him two.
And if anyone asks you for something, give it to him. And do not refuse to lend to anyone who asks you to borrow. Love of all.
You have heard that it was said, ‘Love your neighbor and hate your enemy.’ #Love… your enemy. 43:5 From Leviticus 19:18.
But I tell you, love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.
so that you may be children of your Father who is in heaven. For He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love only those who love you, what reward do you deserve? Do not even tax collectors do the same?
And if you care for your brothers only, what distinguishes you from others? Do not even the idolaters do the same?
Therefore be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving.
Beware of practicing your righteousness before other people to be seen by them. Otherwise, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
So when you give to the needy, do not announce it with trumpets as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and on the streets, to be praised by men. Truly, I say to you, they have received their reward in full.
But when you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing,
So that your giving may be in secret. And your Father who sees what happens in secret will reward you. Prayer.
And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the corners of the streets, that they may be seen by others. Truly, I say to you, they have received their reward in full.
But when you pray, go into your room and shut the door, and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees in secret will reward you.
And when you pray, do not utter meaningless phrases as the pagans do, for they think that by repeating their prayers at length, they will be answered.
Therefore, do not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.
Therefore pray like this: ‘Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name,
Your kingdom come, your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
And forgive us our sins, as we have also forgiven those who wrong us.
And do not put us to the test, but deliver us from evil. #evil. 13:6 Satan (Iblis). For Yours is the kingdom and the power and the glory, forever and ever. Amen.
For if you forgive others their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive the mistakes of others, your Father will not forgive your mistakes. Fasting.
And when you fast, do not look sullen like the hypocrites. For they disfigure their faces that they may appear to others to be fasting. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward in full.
But when you fast, do not put oil on your head, nor wash your face,
So that people do not notice that you are fasting. For your Father who does not see you, sees it. Then your Father who sees what happens in secret will reward you. God or money.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy and where thieves break in and steal.
But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where moths and rust do not destroy, and where thieves do not break in and steal.
For your heart will be where your treasure is.
The lamp of the body is the eye. If your eye is healthy, your whole body will be full of light.
But if your eye is evil, your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, how great will the darkness be?
No one can serve two masters. For he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and wealth. #Wealth. 24:6 Literally “Mammon.” It is an Aramaic word meaning “riches,” in its negative sense, as it represents here a god that people serve instead of God. Seek first the kingdom of God.
Therefore I tell you, do not worry about your livelihood, that is, about what you will eat and drink. Nor worry about your body, that is, about what you will wear. For life is more important than food, and the body is more important than clothing.
Look at the birds of the sky, they do not sow or reap, nor gather into barns, yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not more valuable than they?
Who among you can add an hour to their life when they worry?
And why do you worry about what you will wear? Consider how the lilies of the field grow. They do not toil nor do they spin.
But I tell you, not even Solomon in all his glory was dressed like one of these.
If God so clothes the grass of the field, which is here today and tomorrow is thrown into the oven, will he not much more care for you, O you of little faith?
Therefore do not worry or ask yourselves: ‘What will we eat?’ or ‘What will we drink?’ or ‘What will we wear?’
These are the things that people of the world to come seek, and your heavenly Father knows that you need all of them.
But seek first his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.
Do not worry about tomorrow, for each day has enough trouble of its own, and tomorrow will have its own troubles.
Judging others
Do not judge others, so that you may not be judged.
For He will judge you in the same way you judge others, and with the measure you use, it will be measured back to you.
Why do you see the speck in your brother’s eye, but do not notice the log in your own eye?
And how can you say to your brother, “Let me remove the speck from your eye,” while there is a large beam in your own eye?
You hypocrite! First remove the beam from your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother’s eye.
Do not give dogs what is holy, and do not throw your pearls before pigs! For they will trample them under their feet, and turn and tear you in pieces. Persistence in asking.
Ask and it will be given to you, seek and you will find, knock and the door will be opened to you.
For everyone who asks receives, and everyone who seeks finds, and for everyone who knocks, the door will be opened.
Which of you, if your son asks for bread, will give him a stone?
Or if he asks for a fish, will they give him a live one?
You, despite your wickedness, know how to give good gifts to your children. Isn’t the Father who is in heaven much more likely to give good gifts to those who ask Him? The Golden Rule.
As you wish others to treat you, so you must treat them. This is the essence of the Law of Moses and the teaching of the prophets. The way of Heaven and the way of Hell.
Enter through the narrow gate, which leads to heaven. For the gate that leads to destruction is wide, and the way is easy, and many there are who enter by it.
But the gate to life is narrow, and the way is difficult, and few are those who find it. A warning against false teachings.
Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly are ravenous wolves.
You will know them by their deeds. People do not gather grapes from thorn bushes, nor figs from thistles!
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit, and every bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit.
And every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
Therefore you will know the false prophets by their fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, ‘Lord, Lord,’ will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: ‘Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not drive out demons in your name? Did we not do many miracles in your name?’
Then I will tell them clearly: I have never known you. Away from me, you evildoers. The wise man and the foolish man.
Anyone who listens to these teachings of mine and acts on them is like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
So the rain fell, and the floodwaters rose, and the wind blew and struck that house, but it did not fall, because its foundation was on the rock.
And whoever listens to my words and does not act upon them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
Then the rain fell, the floodwaters rose, and the wind blew and struck that house, so it collapsed with a tremendous crash!
And when Jesus had finished these sayings, the people were astonished at his teaching,
because He was teaching them with authority, and not as their teachers of the law.
Jesus Cleanses a Leper
Then Jesus went down from the mountain, and a large crowd followed him.
And a man covered in leprosy came to him, knelt before him, and said, “Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean.”
And Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying, “I want to; be clean.” Immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
Then Jesus said to him, “Do not tell anyone what has happened to you; go, show yourself to the priest, #go… to the priest. 4:8 The priest was the one who, according to the law, determined when a leper was considered clean. And offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded, to testify to them that you have been healed.” Faith of a Roman officer.
And Jesus entered Capernaum, and a Roman officer came to Him. #Roman officer. 5:8 Literally “centurion.” Repeated in verses 8, 13.
He said: “My lord, my servant is very ill and bedridden at home. He is immobile and suffering from severe pain.”
And Jesus said to him, “I will go and heal him.”
The officer replied, “Sir, I am not worthy of you entering my home. All you need to do is say a word, and my servant will be healed.”
“For I myself am a man under authority, with soldiers under my command. I tell this soldier, ‘Go!’ and he goes. And I tell another, ‘Come!’ and he comes. And I say to my servant, ‘Do this!’ and he does it.”
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who were following him, “Truly I tell you, I have not found such faith even in Israel.”
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west and recline at table with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven.
But those who should have inherited the kingdom will be thrown into outer darkness. There they will weep and gnash their teeth!”
Then Jesus said to the centurion, “Go, let it be done for you as you have believed.” And his servant was healed at that very moment. “He took up our infirmities”
And when Jesus came into Peter’s house, he saw Peter’s mother-in-law lying in bed with a high fever.
So Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she got up and began to serve Him.
And on that evening, people brought to him many who were possessed by evil spirits, and he cast out the spirits with a word of command, and healed all who were sick.
This happened so that what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled: “He took up our infirmities and carried our diseases.” Follow Jesus.
Now when Jesus saw a large crowd around him, he told his disciples to go to the other side of the lake.
Then one of the teachers of the law came to him and said to him, “Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go.”
And Jesus said to him, “The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head.”
And another of his disciples said to him, “Lord, let me first go and bury my father.”
But Jesus said to him, “Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead.” Jesus calms the storm.
And Jesus got into the boat, and his disciples followed him.
Then a violent storm came up on the lake, so that the boat was swamped with waves. But Jesus was sleeping.
So they came close to him and woke him, saying, “Lord, save us, for we are drowning.”
Then Jesus said to them, “Why are you afraid, you of little faith?” And he rose and rebuked the winds and the sea; and there was a great calm.
But they were amazed and said, “Who is this man, that even the wind and the sea obey him?” Jesus drove out demons from two men.
Then Jesus arrived in the region of the Gadarenes, across the lake, and two men possessed by evil spirits came out of the tombs to meet him. These men were dangerous, and no one dared to travel along that road.
And the evil spirits cried out: “What do you want from us, Son of God? Have you come here to torment us before the appointed time?”
And there was a large herd of pigs grazing at a distance from them.
And the evil spirits pleaded with him, saying, “If you cast us out, we will go into a herd of pigs.”
Then Jesus said, “Go.” And the unclean spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The whole herd, then, rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned in the water.
So the shepherds fled to the town and told the people what had happened to those possessed by evil spirits.
So the whole town came out to see Jesus. And when they saw Him, they begged Him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a paralyzed man.
So Jesus got into a boat to cross to the other side of the lake, and arrived in his town.
So some people brought to him a paralyzed man lying on a mat. When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, “Take heart, son; your sins are forgiven.”
Then some of the teachers of the law said, “This man is blaspheming.”
And Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them, “Why are you thinking evil thoughts in your hearts?”
Which is easier to hear: “Your sins are forgiven” or “Get up and walk”?
But I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins.” And He said to the man who was paralyzed, “Get up, pick up your bed, and go home!”
So he got up and went to his home.
And when the people saw this, they were filled with awe, and they glorified God because He had given such authority to men. When will Jesus follow?
And as Jesus passed by, he saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax booth. And Jesus said to him, “Follow me!” And he rose and followed him.
And while he was eating in Matthew’s house, tax collectors and many sinners came and were eating with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to his disciples, “Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?”
When Jesus heard this, he said, “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick.”
So go and understand what the book means when it says: ‹I want mercy for people, not animal sacrifices.› #I want … animal. 13:9 From the book of Hosea 6: 6. “I did not come to call the righteous, but to call sinners.” A question about fasting.
Then John’s disciples came to him and asked, “Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not?”
Then Jesus said to them, “Can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while the bridegroom is with them? But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.”
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth. For the new piece will pull away from the old, and a worse tear is made.
And no one puts new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wineskins will burst, the wine will spill, and the wineskins will be ruined. So new wine is put into new wineskins, and both are preserved.” Jesus raises a dead girl // and heals a woman who had been bleeding.
While he was speaking, a synagogue leader came forward and knelt before him, saying, “My daughter is dying. Come and lay your hand on her, and she will live.”
So Jesus got up and went with the synagogue leader, and his disciples followed him.
And there was a woman who had suffered from a hemorrhage for twelve years. She came up behind Him and touched the fringe of His cloak.
And she said to herself, “If only I touch his cloak, I will be made well.”
So Jesus turned around and saw her and said to her, “Take heart, daughter, your faith has healed you.” And the woman was healed immediately.
And when Jesus came to the house of the synagogue leader, he saw a commotion, and people weeping and wailing loudly.
He said: “Go outside. The girl hasn’t died, but she is sleeping.” And they laughed at him.
And when the people had gone out of the house, Jesus went to the girl’s room and took her by the hand, and she got up.
And the news about Jesus spread throughout that region. Jesus healed two blind men and cast out an evil spirit.
And after Jesus had departed from that place, two blind men followed Him, crying out, “Son of David, have mercy on us!”
And when Jesus entered the house, two blind men came to Him. And Jesus said to them, “Do you believe that I am able to heal you?” And they said, “Yes, Lord.”
Then He touched their eyes and said, “May you both have what you believed for.”
So the blind men regained their sight. Then Jesus sternly warned them, saying, “Do not let anyone know what has happened to you.”
But they went and spread the news throughout that region.
And as they were going, people brought to Him a man who was mute, possessed by a demon.
Then Jesus cast out the evil spirit, and the mute man began to speak. The people were amazed and said, “Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel.”
But the Pharisees said, “He drives out demons by the power of Beelzebul, #Beelzebul. 34:9 One of the names of Satan, the prince of those spirits.” The harvest is plentiful.
And Jesus went through every city and village, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom. He healed every disease and every sickness among the people.
And when Jesus saw the crowds, he had compassion for them, because they were harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
And Jesus said to his disciples, “The harvest is plentiful, but the workers are few.”
Pray to the Lord of the Harvest to send two harvests to the harvest.
Jesus sends his disciples
And Jesus called his twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits, to cast them out and to heal all disease and sickness.
And these are the names of the twelve apostles: first Simon, who is also called Peter, and his brother Andrew, James the son of Zebedee and his brother John,
Phillips and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the tax collector, James son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus,
Simon, who was also called "the Zealot," #TheZealot. 4:10 from a Jewish political party resisting Roman rule, called the party of "the Zealots." And Judas Iscariot, who betrayed Jesus.
And Jesus sent out these twelve and instructed them, saying: “Do not go into Gentile territory, or enter a Samaritan town,
Rather, go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.
And they proclaimed that the kingdom of heaven is at hand.
Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse those who have leprosy, drive out demons. You have received power to do these things freely, so give freely to others.
Do not carry money made of gold, silver, or copper in your belts.
Do not carry a bag or an extra garment, or an extra pair of shoes, or a staff. The worker deserves his provision.
And when you enter any city or village, seek out someone worthy to receive you, and stay with him until you depart from that city.
And when you enter a house, greet the people of the house.
If the People of the House are worthy, then your salutations will reach them. And if they are not worthy, then your salutations will return to you.
If anyone does not welcome you in a house or in a city, and does not listen to your message, then leave that house or that city, and shake the dust off your feet.
I tell you the truth, on the day of judgment, the men of Sodom and Gomorrah will have a more tolerable condition than that city. Jesus warns of tribulations.
Behold, I send you out as sheep in the midst of wolves. So be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
Beware of people, for they will hand you over to courts and you will be flogged in their synagogues.
They will hand you over to governors and kings for my sake, to bear witness to them and to the Gentiles.
And when they seize you, do not worry about what you will say, for you will be given the right speech at that hour.
Remember that it is not you who will be speaking, but the spirit of your father who will be speaking in you.
A brother will hand over his brother to death, and a father will hand over his son. And children will turn against their parents and kill them.
And they will hate you because of my name, but whoever perseveres to the end will be saved.
And when they persecute you in one city, flee to another. I tell you the truth, you will not finish going through the cities of Israel until the Son of Man comes again.
No student is better than his teacher, nor a slave greater than his master.
Let the student be content to become like his teacher, and the servant to become like his master. If they call the head of the house ‘Beelzebul,’ #Beelzebul – 25:10 – one of the names of Satan, a reference to Matthew 9:34 – what will they call the rest of the members of the house? Fear God, not people.
So do not fear them, for nothing hidden will remain hidden, and nothing secret will remain unrevealed.
And everything I tell you in the darkness, say it in the light, and everything whispered in ears, proclaim it from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body, but cannot kill the soul; rather, fear Him who can destroy both body and soul in hell.
Are not two sparrows sold for a penny? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground apart from your Father’s will.
And as for you, even the hairs of your head are all numbered.
So do not fear, for you are more valuable than many sparrows. Confessing Christ before people.
Whoever confesses me before others, I will also confess him before my Father who is in heaven.
And whoever denies Me before men, I will deny him before My Father who is in heaven. Christ first.
Do not think that I came to establish peace on earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword!
I have come: ‹for a man to disagree with his father, and a daughter with her mother, and a daughter-in-law with her mother-in-law.
So, the enemies of man are the people of his house!
For whoever loves father or mother more than Me is not worthy of Me. And whoever loves son or daughter more than Me is not worthy of Me.
Whoever does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever tries to keep their life will lose it, but whoever loses their life for my sake will keep it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me, and he who welcomes me welcomes the One who sent me.
He who welcomes a prophet as a prophet will receive a prophet’s reward. And he who welcomes a righteous person as righteous will receive a righteous person’s reward.
“And anyone who gives even a cup of cold water to one of these little ones who is a disciple because he is my disciple, I tell you truly, he will certainly not lose his reward.”
John the Baptist and Jesus
And when Jesus had finished teaching his twelve disciples, he departed from that place and went to teach and proclaim his message in the region of Galilee. Jesus and John the Baptist.
While John the Baptist was in prison, he heard about all that the Messiah was doing, and sent a message by his disciples.
And I asked him: “Are you the one we are waiting for, or should we wait for another?”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “Go and tell John what you have heard and seen:
And here the blind see, and the lame walk, and the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, and the dead rise, and the poor have the gospel preached to them.
And congratulations to those who do not hesitate to believe in me.”
And when John’s disciples departed, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John, saying, “What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swaying in the wind?”
Why did you go out then? To see a man wearing fine clothes? Those who wear fine clothes live in the palaces of kings.
So why did you go out? To see a prophet? He is. But I tell you that you have seen one greater than a prophet!
“This is what was written about: ‘See, I will send my messenger ahead of you to clear your way.’”
“I tell you the truth, among those born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist; yet the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.”
From the time of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven has been violently assaulted, and the violent have been trying to seize it by force.
For the law of Moses and all the prophets foretold events until the time of John.
If you want to accept what the prophets and the Law say, John is Elijah, who they prophesied would come. #Elijah … his coming. 14:11 Elijah was one of God’s prophets around 850 BC. And the Jews were expecting his coming based on Malachi 4:5-6.
"He who has ears, let him hear."
What is this generation like? It is like children sitting in marketplaces, calling out to their companions and saying:
We chanted for you, but you did not dance. And we sang funeral songs for you, but you did not wail!
For John had come, neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and people said, ‘He has a demon.’
Then came the Son of Man, eating and drinking wine as others do, and people said about Him, ‘Look at this man, a glutton and a wine drinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ But wisdom is proved right by its fruits.” Jesus warns the wicked cities.
Then Jesus began to denounce the cities in which most of his miracles had been performed, because their people had not repented of their sins.
And he said: “Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles had been performed in Tyre and Sidon that were performed in you, they would have repented long ago, and put on sackcloth and sat in ashes.”
But I tell you that the condition of the people of Tyre and Sidon will be more bearable than yours on the day of judgment.
And you, Capernaum, do you imagine that you will be lifted up to heaven? No, but you will descend to hell! For if the miracles that took place in you had taken place in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you that the fate of Sodom will be more tolerable on the day of judgment than yours.” Jesus is the source of comfort.
At that time Jesus spoke and said: “I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth. For you have hidden these things from the wise and intelligent, and have revealed them to infants.”
Yes, Father, because you were pleased to do this.
The Father has entrusted everything to me. No one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son and anyone to whom the Son chooses to reveal him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Take my yoke upon you. #yoke. 29:11 The yoke is a wooden device placed on animals to help them carry heavy loads, and is a Jewish symbol of the law. Come to me, and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
“For my yoke is easy and my burden is light.”
Jesus: Lord of the Sabbath
At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath. His disciples were hungry and began to pick heads of grain and eat them.
But when the Pharisees saw it, they said to him, “Look, your disciples are doing what is not lawful on the Sabbath.”
Then Jesus said to them, “Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, he and those who were with him?”
He entered the house of God and ate from the loaves of bread offered to God, even though neither he nor those with him are permitted to eat that bread, but only the priests are allowed to do so.
Have you not also read in the Law of Moses that the priests on the Sabbath break the Sabbath law? Yet they are not held accountable for doing so.
But I tell you, there is something greater than the temple here.
And if you knew what the Book means when it says: ‹I want mercy for people, not animal sacrifices.› #I want… animal. 7:12 From the Book of Hosea 6:6. How you judged those innocents.
“For the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.” Healing occurred on the Sabbath.
Then he left that place and went to their gathering.
And there was a man with a paralyzed hand. They asked Jesus, “Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?” They asked this in order to find an accusation against him.
He said to them, “Suppose one of you has a sheep that falls into a pit on the Sabbath, would you not grasp it and pull it out?”
And a human being is more important than a sheep. Therefore, doing good on Saturday is in accordance with Sharia.”
Then Jesus said to the man with the withered hand, “Stretch out your hand.” And he stretched it out, and it was completely restored, as good as his other hand.
Then the Pharisees went and plotted how to trap Jesus. Jesus: God’s chosen servant.
So Jesus knew their plan and left that place. A large crowd followed him, and he healed them all.
And he ordered them not to reveal who he is.
This happened so that what was spoken by God through the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled:
Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom I have taken delight. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will proclaim justice to the nations.
He will not quarrel with anyone, nor will he shout, and no one will hear his voice in their streets.
It will not break even a bent reed, nor extinguish even a smoldering wick. And it will continue until justice prevails.
“And all nations will hope in Him.” Jesus reigns.
Then they brought to Him a blind and mute man possessed by a demon, and He healed him. So the mute man spoke and saw.
And the people were amazed and said: “Can this be the son of David?”
Now when the Pharisees heard this, they said, “This man casts out demons by the power of Beelzebul. #Beelzebul. 24:12 One of the names of Satan. Also in verse 27. The prince of the demons.”
Now Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them, “Any kingdom divided against itself will fall, and any house divided against itself will not stand.”
So if Satan casts out demons and wages war against himself, how can his kingdom stand?
If I drive out demons by Beelzebul, by what power do your exorcists drive them out? They are the ones who condemn you.
But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
How can anyone enter a strong man’s house and plunder his possessions, unless he first ties up the strong man? Then he is able to plunder his house.
He who is not with me is against me. And he who does not gather with me scatters.
Therefore I tell you, every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven people, but blasphemy against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will never be forgiven, neither in this world nor in the world to come. The fruit reveals the truth.
To get good fruit, plant a good tree. But a bad tree will give you bad fruit. For a tree is known by its fruit.
You offspring of vipers, how can you speak good things while you yourselves are evil? For the mouth speaks what the heart is full of.
Therefore, the good person brings forth good things from his store of good, and the evil person brings forth evil things from his store of evil.
But I tell you that on the day of judgment people will give account for every careless word they speak.
"Your words will determine your innocence or conviction." Jewish leaders demand proof.
Then some of the teachers of the law and Pharisees challenged him, saying, “Teacher, show us a sign to prove your authority.”
He answered them, “This wicked and adulterous generation seeks a sign; and no sign will be given to it except the sign of the prophet Jonah.”
For just as Jonah was in the belly of the great fish for three days and three nights, so will the Son of Man be in the heart of the earth for three days and three nights.
The people of Nineveh will stand with this generation at the judgment and condemn it, because they repented at the preaching of Jonah. And now, someone greater than Jonah is here among you.
And the Queen of the South will rise up #QueenOfTheSouth. 42:12 Queen of Sheba. She traveled nearly two thousand kilometers to hear the wisdom of God on the lips of King Solomon. See 1 Kings 10:1-13. The day of judgment against this generation and it will condemn it. For she came from the ends of the earth to hear Solomon’s wisdom. And now here before you is one greater than Solomon. The empty soul.
When an unclean spirit goes out of a person, it passes through dry places seeking rest, and finds none.
Then he says, ‘I will return to my house from which I came.’ So he goes and finds the house empty, swept, and put in order.
Then it goes and brings with it seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter and dwell there. And so the last state of that person is worse than his first. So it will happen to this present evil generation.” Jesus’ disciples are his family.
And while Jesus was speaking to the crowds, His mother and brothers stood outside and sought to speak with Him.
Then someone said to Jesus, “Your mother and brothers are standing outside and want to speak with you.”
And Jesus answered, “Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?”
Then he pointed with his hand to his disciples and said: “These are my mother and my brothers,”
For whoever does the will of my Father who is in heaven is my brother and sister and mother.”
Like seeds.
On that day, Jesus left the house and sat by the lake shore.
So large crowds gathered around him. He got into a boat and sat in it, while the people stood on the shore.
And He said to them many things in parables. And He said to them: “A farmer went out to sow.
And as he sows, some seed fell by the wayside, and the birds came and ate it.
And some seeds fell on rocky ground, where there was not enough soil, so the seedlings sprouted quickly because the soil was not deep.
But when the sun rose, it burned, and because it had no roots, it withered.
And some seed fell among thorns, and the thorns grew up and hindered its growth.
And other seeds fell on good soil and produced, some a hundredfold, some sixtyfold, and some thirtyfold.
“He who has ears, let him hear.” Listening and understanding.
Then his disciples came to him and asked, “Why do you speak to them in parables?”
And Jesus answered them, “To you it has been given to know the secrets of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it has not been given.
For whoever has, more will be given to him, and he will have abundance, but from whoever does not have, even what he has will be taken away. 12:13 Perhaps what is meant is “whoever possesses understanding.”
That is why I speak to them in parables. Though they see, they do not perceive; though they hear, they do not understand.
And so the prophecy of Isaiah is fulfilled on them: “You will keep listening, but you will not understand. You will keep looking, but you will not see.”
And so the mind of this people has become dull, and their hearing heavy. They have closed their eyes, unable to see with their eyes, or hear with their ears, or understand with their hearts, so that they might not turn back to Me and I heal them.
But as for you, blessed are your eyes for they see, and your ears for they hear.
I tell you the truth, many kings and righteous people have longed to see what you see, but did not see it, and desired to hear what you hear, but did not hear it. It is like the parable of the seed.
So listen to the explanation of the parable:
When a person hears the message of the Kingdom and does not understand it, the wicked one #the wicked one comes. 19:13 That is, Satan. (Also in verse 38) And he takes away the seed that was sown in his heart. This is the meaning of the seed that fell by the roadside.
And as for the one that fell on rocky ground, it is like someone who hears the word and receives it with joy at once,
But because it has no root in itself, it endures for a time, and when tribulation or persecution arises because of the word, it immediately falls away.
But the one that fell among the thorns is like someone who hears the word, but the worries of this life, and the deceitfulness of wealth, choke the word, so it doesn’t bear fruit.
But the one who sowed on good soil is the one who hears the word and understands it, and bears fruit—some a hundredfold, some sixtyfold, some thirtyfold. Like wheat and weeds.
And he told them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field.
But while people were sleeping, the man’s enemy came and sowed weeds among the wheat, then left.
And when the wheat sprouted and formed ears, the weeds sprouted as well.
Then his servants came to him and said, “Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then did these weeds come from?”
Then the man answered them, “My enemy did this.” And his servants asked him, “Shall we go and uproot it?”
And the man replied: “No, for when you uproot the weeds, you risk uprooting the wheat with them.”
“Let both grow together until the time of harvest; then I will tell the harvesters, ‘First gather the weeds and bind them in bundles to be burned, but gather the wheat into my barn.’” For example, mustard and yeast.
Then Jesus told them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field.”
It is the smallest of seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest of garden plants, becoming a great tree, so much so that the birds of the sky come to it and make their nests in its branches.
And he told them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till it was all leavened.”
Jesus used parables to teach all these things. He did not speak to the people except in parables.
So this is what God said on the tongue of the Prophet: “I will open My mouth in parables, and I will declare things hidden since the creation of the world.”
Then Jesus sent the crowds away and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, “Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field.”
And He said to them: “The one who sows the good seed is the Son of Man.”
And the field is the world, and the good seeds are those who belong to the kingdom. But the weeds are those who belong to the evil one.
And the one who sowed them is the devil. And the harvest is the end of the world. And the harvesters are the angels.
And just as weeds are gathered and burned with fire, so too will be the end of the world.
For the Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom all obstacles and those who do evil,
Then they throw them into the blazing furnace. There, people weep and gnash their teeth.
Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears, let them hear. The parable of the treasure and the pearl.
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure buried in a field, which a man found and reburied, and in his joy went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
And the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
And when he found a pearl of great value, he went and sold all that he had and bought it. Like a fishing net.
And the kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the sea, and it gathered fish of every kind.
And when the net was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat and began to select the good fish and put it in baskets, while the bad fish they threw away.
This is what will happen at the end of the world, for the angels will come and separate the wicked from the righteous.
Then the wicked will be thrown into the blazing furnace. There they will weep and gnash their teeth.”
And Jesus asked his disciples, “Do you understand all these things?” They replied, “Yes.”
So he said to them, “Therefore every teacher of the law who has become a disciple of the kingdom of heaven is like the owner of a house who brings out of his storehouse new treasures as well as old.” And Jesus went to his hometown.
And when Jesus had finished relating those parables, he departed from that place.
And when He went to His hometown, He began to teach them in their synagogue. And they were all amazed and said, “Where did this man get this wisdom and these miracles?”
Isn't he the carpenter's son? Isn't his mother Mary? Aren't his brothers James and Joseph and Simon and Judas?
Don't all his sisters live among us? Where did he get everything he has?
So this was an obstacle preventing them from accepting it. But Jesus said to them, “A prophet is not without honor except in his hometown and in his own house!”
He did not perform many miracles there, because of their lack of faith.
Herod hears about Jesus.
At that time, Herod, the tetrarch of Galilee – #tetrarch of Galilee. 1:14 literally “ruler of a quarter.” The Romans had divided Palestine into four provinces, so the ruler of each province was called a tetrarch or ruler of a quarter. See the Gospel of Luke 3:1. about Jesus.
So he said to his servants, “It is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and that is why the miracles are happening through him!” The Death of John the Baptist
Herod was the one who arrested John and put him in prison, and this was because of Herodias, the wife of his brother Philip.
For John had said to Herod, “It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife.”
This is why Herod wanted to kill him, but he was afraid of the crowd, because they considered John a prophet.
But when Herod’s birthday came, the daughter of Herodias danced before him and his guests, and pleased Herod greatly.
He even promised and swore to give her whatever she asked for, no matter what it was.
But her mother had instructed her what to ask for, so she said, “Bring me John the Baptist’s head here on a platter.”
The king was saddened, but he ordered her request to be fulfilled due to his oath, and out of respect for his guests.
So he sent and had John the Baptist beheaded in prison.
Then he brought her his head on a platter and gave it to her, and she gave it to her mother.
Then John’s disciples came and took his body and buried it. Then they went and told Jesus what had happened. Jesus feeds more than five thousand.
And when Jesus heard this, He got into a boat and went to a secluded place. The crowds found out about it and went after Him on foot from the cities.
And when He went down to the seashore, He saw a great crowd, and He had compassion on them and healed their sick.
In the evening, his disciples came to him and said, “This place is isolated and it is very late. Send the people away to go to the villages and buy themselves food.”
But Jesus said to them, “There is no need for them to go; you give them something to eat.”
Then his disciples said to him, “We have here only five loaves of bread and two fish.”
And Jesus said to them, “Bring her to me.”
And he commanded the people to sit on the grass. Then Jesus took the five loaves and the two fish, and thanked God, looking up to heaven. Then he broke them and gave the loaves to his disciples to distribute to the people.
So they ate and were all filled. And they gathered up the leftover fragments, and there were twelve baskets full of broken pieces.
And the number of those who ate was five thousand men, not counting women and children. Jesus walks on water.
Then Jesus told his disciples to get into the boat and go ahead of him to the other side, while he dismissed the crowd.
And after dismissing them, he went up the mountain alone to pray. When evening came, he was there alone.
The boat had reached the middle of the lake, and the waves were crashing against it violently, as the wind was blowing against the boat’s direction.
And just before dawn, Jesus came to them, walking on the sea.
When his disciples saw him walking on the lake, they were terrified and said, “It is a ghost!” And they cried out in fear.
And Jesus immediately said to them, “Take courage, it is I, do not be afraid.”
Then Peter answered Him, “Lord, if it is You, command me to come to You on the water.”
So He said to him, “Come.” And Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water and went toward Jesus.
But when Peter became aware of the strong wind, he was afraid and began to sink, and cried out: “Lord, save me.”
And Jesus immediately reached out his hand and touched him, saying, “O you of little faith, why did you doubt?”
And when Jesus and Peter got into the boat, the wind stopped.
And those who were in the boat worshiped Him and said, “You are truly the Son of God.” Jesus healed many.
And when they crossed the lake, they reached the region of Gennesaret.
Now when the inhabitants of that region knew Jesus, they sent out into all the surrounding country, bringing to Him all who were sick,
And they implored Him to allow them to touch even the fringe of His garment. And all who touched it were healed.
God’s law and people’s traditions
Then some Pharisees and teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to Jesus and asked him:
Why do your students break the traditions we inherited from our ancestors? They don't wash their hands before eating.
And Jesus answered them, “Why do you yourselves break the commandment of God for the sake of your tradition?”
For God has commanded and said: ‹Honor your father and your mother,› #Honor… your mother. 4:15 From the Book of Exodus 20: 12, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5: 16. And He said: ‹Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death.› #Whoever curses… shall be put to death. 4:15 From the Book of Exodus 21: 17.
But you say, ‘Whoever says to his father or mother, “I cannot help you, because everything I have I have given to the Lord,” is not obligated to honor his father or mother.’
And in doing so, you have disregarded God’s commandment because of your traditions.
O you hypocrites, Isaiah rightly prophesied about you when he said:
This people honor me with their lips, but their hearts are far from me.
their worship is useless, because they teach teachings that are nothing but human commandments.
And Jesus called the people to Him and said to them: “Listen to Me and understand what I say.”
It is not what enters the mouth that defiles a person, but what comes out of the mouth; this is what defiles him.”
Then the disciples came to him and said, “Do you realize that the Pharisees were offended when they heard what you said?”
And Jesus answered, “Every plant that my Father has not planted will be uprooted.”
Leave them; they are leading the blind. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.”
Then Peter replied, “Explain the meaning of this parable to us.”
Then Jesus said, “Do you still not understand?”
Do you not understand that whatever goes into the mouth enters the stomach, and then goes out into the latrine?
But what comes out of a person’s mouth comes from the heart. This is what defiles a person.
For it is from the heart that evil thoughts come, murder, adultery, sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, and slander.
These are the things that defile a person, but eating with unwashed hands does not make a person defiled.” Jesus helps a strange woman.
And Jesus left that place and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
And a Canaanite woman from that region came to him, and began to cry out, “Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David! My daughter is possessed by a demon, and she is suffering greatly.”
But Jesus answered her not a word. Then came his disciples, and entreated him, saying, “Send her away, for she cries after us.”
And He said: “I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.”
But the woman approached him and knelt before him, and said, “Lord, help me.”
And Jesus answered her, “It is not good to take the children’s bread and throw it to the dogs.”
And she said, “True, sir, but even dogs eat the scraps from their owners’ table.”
At that moment Jesus answered her, “Woman, your faith is great! Be it to you as you desire.” And at once her daughter was healed. Jesus heals many.
And Jesus left that place and went to a region near the Sea of Galilee. He went up on a hillside and sat there.
Then large crowds came to him, and they brought the lame, the blind, the paralyzed, the mute and deaf, and many other sick people. They placed them at Jesus’ feet, and he healed them.
And the crowds were amazed when they saw the deaf speaking, the lame walking, the paralyzed healed, and the blind seeing, so they praised the God of Israel. Jesus feeds more than four thousand.
Then Jesus called his disciples and said, “I have compassion for these people; they have been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way.”
Then his disciples asked him, “Where shall we get enough bread to feed this crowd in such a remote place?”
Then Jesus asked them, “How many loaves do you have?” They replied, “Seven—and a few small fish.”
Then Jesus directed the people to sit down on the ground.
And he took the seven loaves and the fish, and gave thanks, and broke the loaves and gave them to his disciples to distribute to the crowd.
So everyone ate and was full. Then they collected what was left of the broken pieces, and there were seven baskets full.
The number of those who ate was four thousand men, not counting women and children.
And when Jesus dismissed the crowds, he got into a boat and went to the region of Magdala.
Jewish leaders examine Jesus.
Then the Pharisees and Sadducees came to Jesus and tested him, asking him to show them a sign from God.
And Jesus answered them, “When evening comes, you say, ‘The weather will be fair, for the sky is red.’
And in the early morning, you say: “Today will be stormy, for the sky is red and gloomy.” You are skilled at interpreting the signs of the weather, but you are not skilled at understanding the times in which you live!
This wicked and adulterous generation seeks a sign to believe. And no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah.” Then Jesus left them and went away. Lack of understanding.
And Jesus’ disciples crossed to the other side of the lake, but they forgot to bring bread.
And Jesus said to them, “Beware and be on your guard against the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”
Then the disciples began to talk among themselves, saying, “But we forgot to bring bread!”
So Jesus knew what they were saying to one another, and he said to them, “Why are you discussing among yourselves the fact that you have no bread?”
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand, and how many baskets you collected of the broken pieces?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand, and how many baskets you gathered of broken pieces?
Why do you not understand that I did not speak to you about bread in the ordinary sense, but rather I was warning you to guard yourselves from the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees?”
Then his disciples understood that he had not warned them to beware of the leaven of bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter declares that Jesus is the Christ.
And when Jesus came into the region of Caesarea Philippi, He asked His disciples, “Who do people say that I am, the Son of Man?”
And He asked His disciples, “Who do people say that I am?” Some said, “John the Baptist,” and others, “Elijah,” and still others, “Jeremiah, or one of the prophets.”
He said to them, “And you, who do you think I am?”
Then Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”
And Jesus answered him, “Blessed are you, Simon son of John, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.”
And I say to you, you are Peter, #Peter. 18:16 From the Greek "Petros" and it means "rock." Upon this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell #gates of hell. 18:16 That is, the power of death. Will not be able to overcome it.
And I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”
Then he strictly warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about the necessity of his death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests and the teachers of the law. He must be killed and raised to life on the third day.
But Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him, saying, “God forbid, Lord! This shall never happen to you!”
Then Jesus turned and said to Peter, “Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; you do not have in mind the things of God, but the things of men.”
Then Jesus said to his disciples, “If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.”
So whoever wants to save his life will lose it. But whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
What good would it do for someone to gain the whole world, yet forfeit their soul? Or what can someone give to regain their life?
For the Son of Man will come in the glory of his Father with his angels, and then he will repay each person according to what he has done.
“Truly I tell you, there are some standing here who will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.”
Jesus, and with Him Moses and Elijah.
After six days, Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain by themselves.
And while they were there, the appearance of Jesus changed, and his face shone like the sun, and his clothes became white as light.
And suddenly Moses and Elijah appeared before the disciples, and they were talking with Jesus.
Then Peter said to Jesus, “Lord, it is good that we are here! If you wish, I will make three dwellings here, one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.”
While Peter was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice came from the cloud, saying, “This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him.”
And when the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell face down to the ground.
Then Jesus came close and touched them, saying, “Rise, do not fear.”
And when they looked around, they saw no one except Jesus.
And as they were coming down the mountain, Jesus instructed them, “Tell no one what you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead.”
And his disciples asked him, “Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?” #Elijah… first. 10:17 Elijah was one of God’s prophets around 850 BC. And the Jews were expecting his coming based on Malachi 4:5-6.
And Jesus answered them, “Truly, Elijah is coming to restore all things.”
But I tell you that Elijah has already come, and people did not recognize him, but they did to him whatever they wanted. And the Son of Man will also be treated that way by them.”
Then his disciples understood that he was speaking of John the Baptist. Jesus cast out a demon from a boy.
And when they returned to the crowd, a man came to Jesus and knelt before Him.
He said: “Have mercy on my son, O Lord, for he suffers from epilepsy and is in great pain. He often falls into the fire or the water.”
And I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.”
Then Jesus said: “O faithless and twisted generation, how long shall I be with you, how long shall I bear with you?” And to the man He said, “Bring your son here.”
And Jesus commanded the unclean spirit to come out of him, and the boy was healed immediately.
Then his disciples came to him privately and asked, “Why could we not cast it out?”
Jesus replied to them, “Because of your lack of faith. Truly I tell you, if you have faith the size of a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, ‘Move from here to there,’ and it will move, and nothing will be impossible for you.”
“But this kind can only be driven out by prayer and fasting.” Jesus foretells his approaching death.
And as they were traveling through Galilee together, Jesus said to them, “The Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of men.”
And they will kill him, but on the third day he will rise from the dead.” The disciples were very distressed. Temple tax.
And when Jesus entered Capernaum, he came to Peter, who was collecting the two-drachma tax for the temple, and they asked him, “Does your teacher pay the two-drachma tax?”
Then Peter replied to them, “Yes, he will.” And Peter went to the house. Jesus, anticipating him, said, “Tell me, Simon, from whom do kings collect tribute and taxes? From their own people, or from foreigners?”
Then Peter answered, “They collect it from the other peoples.” And Jesus said, “Then the children are exempt from it.”
But so as not to cause them trouble, go to the lake and cast your fishing rod. Then take the first fish you catch, and open its mouth. You will find in it a coin worth four dirhams. Take it and give it to them on my behalf and yours.
